4,"Yabaana Language and the Yabaâna Indian Tribe (Jabâ-ana, Hobacana)",https://www.native-languages.org/yabaana.htm,"Yabaana Indian Language (Jabaana) Yabaana is an extinct Arawakan language of South America, once spoken in Brazil. It is closely related to the still-living Xiriana language. Though it is often referred to as ""Yabarana"" in the literature, it is unrelated to the Venezuelan language Yabarana, which is a Cariban language.",Yabaâna,Maipurean
5,Mainatari language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/mainatari_language,"The Mainatari language, also known as Maihanatari or Yabaâna-Mainatari, is an extinct Arawakan language formerly spoken by indigenous communities in the Orinoco River basin of Venezuela, specifically along the Castaña-Paraná River, a tributary of the Siapa River. [1] Documented primarily through wordlists collected by Austrian naturalist Johann Natterer during his expeditions in the region ...",Yabaâna,Maipurean
